course: dictionary
lesson: 11792
title: Understanding belongings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Belongings are the things you own that you can carry or move, such as clothes, bags and toys. «Ella packed her belongings into her backpack before the trip.» The word packed is a clue: you pack things you own into a bag, so belongings must mean your things.
explain: Belongings is a plural noun, so it names more than one thing. «Please label your belongings so they go back to the right peg.» We use it for a group of things, like your coat, your lunchbox and your pencil case together.
explain: Context helps you here too. «When the fire alarm rang, we left our belongings in the classroom and walked outside.» The alarm and the walk outside tell you that belongings are things you leave behind to stay safe.
explain: Test the meaning by swapping it in. Put «things I own» where belongings was, and check that the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have understood the word.
fact: Belongings means the things that you own and can carry or move.
fact: «Belongings» is a plural noun, so it names more than one thing.
fact: Examples of belongings are clothes, bags, toys and books.
fact: «Personal belongings» means things that belong to one person only, like your own water bottle.
fact: «Pack your belongings» means put your things into bags or boxes, and «gather your belongings» means collect them together.
exercise: «Please label your belongings so they do not get lost.» What are belongings? | Rules that you must follow in class | *The things that you own and can carry or move | Places where you sit to eat lunch | Teachers who look after the school
exercise: «When the fire alarm rang, we left our belongings and walked calmly outside.» Why did the children leave their belongings? | Because belongings are always heavy | *To stay safe and get outside quickly | To give them to the teacher as a present | To wash them in the sink
exercise: Find and copy the word in «Ella packed her belongings into her bag.» that names the things you own. | packed | Ella | *belongings | into
test: «Ella gathered her belongings and left the bus.» What does gathered her belongings mean? | Dropped her hat on the bus | Sang a happy song | Lost her way home | *Collected her things
test: Which of these is a personal belonging? | A school desk in the classroom | A bus timetable on the wall | The roof of the school | *Your own pencil case with your name on it
exam: Which sentence uses belongings correctly? | *Please check you have all your belongings before you leave | Belongings is a loud noise in the hall | We belongings to the park on Sunday | The sun is one of my belongings
